@title Member Profile Database
@subtitle Database choice for the member-profile service, Accounts team, Lattice Fitness
@author Accounts team
@date Decision due 2026-11-04

kpi Profiles | 9M | about 2 KB each
kpi Peak reads | 3,500/s
kpi Peak writes | 220/s

The workload is mostly key lookups, plus a few admin searches by email and gym.

# Comparison

table = Option,Query flexibility,Scaling model,Ops effort,Multi-region; PostgreSQL (RDS),high,vertical plus read replicas,medium,read replicas only; DynamoDB,low,automatic,low,global tables; MongoDB Atlas,medium,sharding,medium,global clusters

# Cost at scale

data cost = traffic,PostgreSQL,DynamoDB,MongoDB Atlas; Today,780,540,910; 3x traffic,1640,1420,1870; 10x traffic,4300,4650,5200
chart bar cost x=traffic y=PostgreSQL,DynamoDB,MongoDB Atlas unit=$ title="Estimated monthly cost by traffic level"

At 3x traffic, PostgreSQL costs $220 a month more than DynamoDB. At 10x it becomes the cheaper of the two.

# Pros and cons

### PostgreSQL (RDS)
- Pro: flexible queries for admin search.
- Pro: cost stays close to DynamoDB at 3x.
- Con: scales vertically, with read replicas for reads.
- Con: multi-region is read replicas only.

### DynamoDB
- Pro: automatic scaling and low ops effort.
- Pro: cheapest today and at 3x traffic.
- Con: low query flexibility for searches by email and gym.

### MongoDB Atlas
- Pro: global clusters for multi-region.
- Pro: medium query flexibility.
- Con: most expensive at every traffic level.
- Con: sharding adds ops effort.

# Recommendation

! Choose **PostgreSQL (RDS)**. Admin search needs flexible queries, and its cost stays within $220 a month of DynamoDB at our realistic 3x horizon.

!! Write volume above 2,000 per second would force us to revisit sharding.
